# Progress-RS

> Beautiful, informative progress bars for Rust applications

[![Crates.io](https://img.shields.io/crates/v/progress-rs)](https://crates.io/crates/progress-rs)
[![Documentation](https://docs.rs/progress-rs/badge.svg)](https://docs.rs/progress-rs)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Build Status](https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/SuperInstance/progress-rs/ci.yml)](https://github.com/SuperInstance/progress-rs/actions)

## 10-Second Hook

**Progress-rs** makes showing task progress beautiful and informative. Build gorgeous progress bars with type-safe templates, smart ETA calculation, and first-class async support.

```rust
use progress_rs::{ProgressBar, Template};
use progress_rs::style::Color;

fn main() {
    let template = Template::builder()
        .bar(40, Color::Cyan, Color::Blue)
        .spacer()
        .percent()
        .build();

    let bar = ProgressBar::with_template(1000, template);

    for _ in 0..1000 {
        do_work();
        bar.inc(1);
    }

    bar.finish();
}
```

## Why Progress-RS?

### Beautiful by Default

Gorgeous progress bars with zero configuration. Smart defaults, great colors, smooth animations.

### Type-Safe Templates

Builder-based template API with compile-time checking. No more error-prone string DSLs.

```rust
// Progress-rs: Type-safe
let template = Template::builder()
    .bar(40, Color::Green, Color::DarkGray)
    .spacer()
    .percent()
    .build();

// Indicatif: String-based (error-prone)
let style = ProgressStyle::default_bar()
    .template("{bar:40} {percent}")
    .unwrap();
```

### Smart ETA

Advanced algorithms that learn from patterns, handle variable workloads, and provide confidence intervals.

### Async-Native

First-class async support. No boilerplate Arc/Mutex dance.

```rust
use progress_rs::ProgressStreamExt;
use futures::stream::{self, StreamExt};

// Automatic progress tracking
let stream = stream::iter(0..1000).progress(1000);

stream.for_each(|item| async move {
    process(item).await;
}).await;
```

### Hierarchical Multi-Progress

Organize multiple bars with nesting, grouping, and grids.

```
Project Build [████████████████████████████] 1000/1000
  Compile [████████████████████████████] 500/500 ✓
  Test [████████████████████████████] 300/300 ✓
  Package [████████████████████████████] 200/200 ✓
```

## Key Features

- ✅ **Beautiful defaults** - Great UX out of the box
- ✅ **Type-safe templates** - Compile-time checked
- ✅ **Smart ETA** - Exponential smoothing, adaptive algorithms
- ✅ **Async-native** - First-class tokio/async-std support
- ✅ **Multi-progress** - Hierarchical layouts, grids, nesting
- ✅ **Rich metrics** - Throughput, speed, acceleration
- ✅ **Cross-platform** - Linux, macOS, Windows, WSL
- ✅ **Persistence** - Save/load progress state
- ✅ **Zero-cost** - Disable for production builds
- ✅ **Iterator adapters** - Progress for any iterator

## Quick Start

### Installation

Add to `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
progress-rs = "0.1"
```

### Basic Usage

```rust
use progress_rs::ProgressBar;

fn main() {
    let bar = ProgressBar::new(1000);

    for _ in 0..1000 {
        do_work();
        bar.inc(1);
    }

    bar.finish();
}
```

### Custom Template

```rust
use progress_rs::{ProgressBar, Template};
use progress_rs::style::Color;

let template = Template::builder()
    .prefix()                    // "Downloading"
    .spacer()
    .bar(40, Color::Cyan, Color::Blue)
    .spacer()
    .percent()
    .spacer()
    .message()                   // "file.txt"
    .build();

let bar = ProgressBar::with_template(1000, template);
bar.set_prefix("Downloading");
bar.set_message("file.txt");

for _ in 0..1000 {
    download_chunk();
    bar.inc(1);
}

bar.finish();
```

**Output**:
```
Downloading [████████████████████████████] 75% file.txt
```

### Multi-Progress

```rust
use progress_rs::MultiProgress;

let multi = MultiProgress::new();

let bar1 = multi.add(100, "Task 1");
let bar2 = multi.add(100, "Task 2");
let bar3 = multi.add(100, "Task 3");

// Run tasks concurrently...
```

**Output**:
```
Task 1 [████████████████████████████] 100/100 ✓
Task 2 [████████████████████░░░░░░░░] 60/100
Task 3 [████████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░] 20/100
```

### Async Iterator

